{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg9xLaxyKun9cDi6zoaSj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"`Recovery` now handles a panic with `http.ErrAbortHandler` the same way it already handles a broken pipe. It logs one short line with no stack trace, doesn't write a 500, records the error on the context with `c.Error` and aborts. The new test passes and the full `go test ./...` is green.\n\n- **`recovery.go`**: 3 added lines. If the panic value is `http.ErrAbortHandler` (or wraps it), it's treated like a broken pipe.\n- **`recovery_test.go`**: new `TestPanicWithAbortHandler`. Before the fix it failed for the expected reason: the response came back as 500 instead of 204, and the full stack trace was logged. Now it passes.\n- **Checks**: `go vet` and `gofmt -l` are clean.\n\n**Why not something bigger:** re-panicking so `net/http` handles the abort itself would be closer to what the standard library intends. But it would change what everything above the middleware sees, so I reused the existing broken-pipe path instead.\n\n**Behaviour change:** with `CustomRecovery`, your own recovery function is no longer called for `ErrAbortHandler`, just as it already isn't for broken pipes. The logger still writes one line without a stack trace, so the error isn't completely silent.\n\nNothing is committed yet, and you're on a detached HEAD. If you want a commit, I'd use your title, `fix(recover): suppress http.ErrAbortHandler in recover`, on a new branch."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":724,"cache_read_input_tokens":38885,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":724,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bcdcd338-91da-4351-b0f4-931d2f4fdd41","uuid"